<div class="path-head"><p class="featured-region">Reading path · 9 stops</p><h1 class="path-title">The Cold War</h1><p class="path-tagline">Forty years of rivalry between the United States and the Soviet Union, from containment to collapse.</p><p class="path-intro">After the Second World War the two superpowers, the United States and the Soviet Union, divided Europe and much of the world between them. They never went to war with each other directly; instead they competed through alliances, an arms race, the race into space and proxy wars in Korea, Vietnam, Africa and elsewhere. This path follows the Cold War from the Truman Doctrine of 1947 through the crises of Berlin and Cuba to the reforms that ended it and the breakup of the Soviet Union in 1991.</p><p class="path-types">Suits <a href="/start">the Strategist</a> · <a href="/start">the Timekeeper</a></p></div>
<ol class="path-steps"><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/truman-doctrine"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/truman-doctrine.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">1 · Encyclopedia · Idea · 1947</span><span class="path-step-name">Truman Doctrine</span><span class="path-step-why">Truman's pledge of March 1947 to support countries under communist pressure, often seen as the start of containment.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/berlin-airlift"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/berlin-airlift.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">2 · Encyclopedia · Event · 1948–1949</span><span class="path-step-name">Berlin Airlift</span><span class="path-step-why">When the Soviets blockaded West Berlin in June 1948, Western aircraft flew in food and coal until the blockade was lifted in May 1949.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/korean-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/korean-war.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">3 · Encyclopedia · Event · 1950–1953</span><span class="path-step-name">Korean War</span><span class="path-step-why">North Korea invaded the South in 1950; a US-led United Nations force and then China's armies joined the war, which ended in an armistice in 1953.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/sputnik-1"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sputnik-1.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">4 · Encyclopedia · Work · 1957</span><span class="path-step-name">Sputnik 1</span><span class="path-step-why">The first artificial satellite, launched in October 1957, shocked the United States and began the space race.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/berlin-wall"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/berlin-wall.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">5 · Encyclopedia · Work · 1961–1989</span><span class="path-step-name">Berlin Wall</span><span class="path-step-why">Built from 13 August 1961 to stop East Germans fleeing west, it was the leading symbol of a divided Europe until its opening in 1989.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/cuban-missile-crisis"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cuban-missile-crisis.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">6 · Encyclopedia · Event · 1962</span><span class="path-step-name">Cuban Missile Crisis</span><span class="path-step-why">Thirteen days in October 1962, over Soviet missile sites in Cuba: the closest the Cold War came to nuclear war.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/prague-spring"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/prague-spring.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">7 · Encyclopedia · Event · 1968</span><span class="path-step-name">Prague Spring</span><span class="path-step-why">Czechoslovakia's 'socialism with a human face', ended when the Soviet Union and its allies invaded in August 1968.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-gorbachev"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mikhail-gorbachev.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">8 · Encyclopedia · Person · 1931–2022</span><span class="path-step-name">Mikhail Gorbachev</span><span class="path-step-why">Glasnost and perestroika at home; abroad he ended the Cold War and allowed the communist regimes of Eastern Europe to fall in 1989.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/dissolution-of-the-soviet-union"><span class="path-step-ph">9</span><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">9 · Encyclopedia · Event · 1991</span><span class="path-step-name">Dissolution of the Soviet Union</span><span class="path-step-why">After a failed coup by hardliners in August 1991 the republics declared independence, and on 26 December the Soviet Union ceased to exist.</span></span></a></li></ol>
